docs/concept/skill-graph/completion-semantics-clarification-2026-11-04.md:155:Tragfähig ist die Regel nur in Einheiten, die die Gültigkeitsregeln R1–R5
docs/concept/skill-graph/source/chapters/03-learning-units.en.md:5:A learning unit connects a learning promise to a coherent path through its goals. When a unit has assessments of its own, it introduces the topic through a dedicated opening and its assessments show what learners can apply independently by the end.
docs/concept/skill-graph/source/chapters/03-learning-units.en.md:8:A **cluster** groups goals. A declared **unit** can be studied as one continuous block in the intended learning flow. A **unit with assessments** demonstrates independent application of its own content goals through its own assessments with reviewed coverage. An assessment-free unit can order goals and child units. Subjects serve as filters on the learning map.
docs/concept/skill-graph/source/chapters/03-learning-units.en.md:17:A unit’s **learning promise** describes what learners will be able to do and why it matters. Every unit with assessments of its own has exactly one **motivation goal** of its own. This is an atomic orientation goal. The backend supplies the coach with the learning promise and the relevant details of the active unit. The coach illustrates its purpose with a concrete situation where possible. For the dot product, the coach might begin: “Your path on the map heads northeast. By the end you can show how much of the journey takes you east and use that to explain the dot product.”
docs/concept/skill-graph/source/chapters/03-learning-units.en.md:19:In the Cockpit the unit opens with a scene, the guiding question, the topics and the learning promise. The learning map shows the current location in its header and the unit’s own motivation goal as the first node in reading order. It is labelled **Introduction** and connects to the first steps on the guided path. Opening a child unit shows its own introduction first. The opening is the first step on the guided path. Content goals, exercises and card practice follow personal completion or valid credit for this orientation. Opening a unit leaves the learner’s results intact. On return the saved progress and next step suffice. Section~@ref{sec:orientation} describes how the coach offers closure.
docs/concept/skill-graph/source/chapters/03-learning-units.en.md:27:**Proposed edge rule.** Every atomic goal the unit owns, apart from orientation goals, reaches its motivation goal along a prerequisite path. This also applies to exercises represented as own goals. The motivation goal requires no content goals. Direct references to it come from within its unit. Starting from the other prerequisites, a tool adds the desired connections and writes direct motivation edges only where no other path provides the connection. This lets the first content goal require both the opening and prior knowledge from a child unit or another subject. The complete graph must be acyclic and transitively minimal.
docs/concept/skill-graph/source/chapters/03-learning-units.en.md:29:**Nested units.** A learner starting the parent and then descending into a child on the guided path encounters both openings in that order. When entering the child directly, its own opening explains the connection to the parent in one sentence. This adds no prerequisite between the two orientation goals. Pure groupings show their location and children. Assessments may be attempted directly. After leaving before submission, guided study resumes with the chosen unit’s outstanding opening.
docs/concept/skill-graph/source/chapters/03-learning-units.en.md:33:At full rollout each content goal, including an experimental one, belongs as an own goal to a unit with motivation and assessments. Its assessments sit in its cluster and cover its own content goals under R1 to R5. A parent synthesis assessment can also require learners to combine content from several children. An aggregate without assessments of its own can connect its children through order alone. During rollout assessment-free units may also order content goals of their own.
docs/concept/skill-graph/source/chapters/03-learning-units.en.md:39:All the atomic goals beneath a unit $U$'s cluster form its **total scope** $T(U)$. The units immediately below it form $\Children(U)$. Each atom in its scope that belongs to none of these child units is an **own goal** of $U$. These goals form $\Own(U)$. Direct child units and own goals together are the unit's **children**. An intervening cluster used purely for grouping is skipped when assigning goals to units.
docs/concept/skill-graph/source/chapters/03-learning-units.en.md:42:T(U) = \Own(U) \cup \bigcup_{V \in \Children(U)} T(V).
docs/concept/skill-graph/source/chapters/03-learning-units.en.md:45:Total scope is non-empty. An aggregate may have no own goals when its children carry the scope. E1 makes each contained atom's smallest unit unique. Overlapping references create neither extra goals nor duplicate progress points. Goals and units without a parent unit are children of an imaginary root. In a full Land plan every goal lies inside one of the two stages; the nationwide canonical structure is no Land plan and may carry goals beside its top units.
docs/concept/skill-graph/source/chapters/03-learning-units.en.md:49:The entry in the placement layer references the cluster and carries the learning promise, revision and any `after` declarations. For a unit with assessments it identifies the coverage reviews of its own assessments. For guided entry the unit references its own motivation goal. References keep the goals and assessment data in their canonical locations.
docs/concept/skill-graph/source/chapters/03-learning-units.en.md:56:| Guided opening (22 November decision) | The three existing subject units already carry a learning promise. | A reference to exactly one own motivation goal per unit with assessments of its own. |
docs/concept/skill-graph/source/chapters/03-learning-units.en.md:57:| Coverage reviews | `coverageReviews`, at most one per own assessment, bound to task and rubric hashes and a review record | Required for units with assessments. |
docs/concept/skill-graph/source/chapters/03-learning-units.en.md:68:The full unit is complete exactly when its own goals are satisfied and all required child units are complete:
docs/concept/skill-graph/source/chapters/03-learning-units.en.md:71:\Complete(U) \iff \bigl(\Own(U) \subseteq \Meff\bigr) \;\land\; \bigwedge_{V \in \Children(U)} \Complete(V).
docs/concept/skill-graph/source/chapters/03-learning-units.en.md:93:In the proposal, $\operatorname{Valid}$ requires an expressly declared, subject-approved contract with real obligations; an empty list is insufficient. $\rho_V$ binds the child's revision. Pure aggregates can bundle real required children. Without an approved direct assessment route, scheduled individual obligations remain necessary. Every independent required exam needs its own valid pass; expressly declared practical or recall obligations apply on both routes. Performing an experiment is no general extra obligation for every unit. Where an individual atom's claim requires own performance, however, neither dialogue nor assessment can evidence that whole atom solely through analysis of supplied data.
docs/concept/skill-graph/source/chapters/03-learning-units.en.md:95:The E.1 trial combines two existing child exams with new own parts A and B. All four are required. A child can release its own ordinary atoms only through its own passed, revision-bound contract; parent completion gains no authority over child or foreign-subject atoms. Details, the deliberate delay in release and open decisions appear in Section~@ref{sec:proposal-unit-assessment}.
docs/concept/skill-graph/source/chapters/03-learning-units.en.md:100:Before the whole unit is complete, a content goal of its own can already count as independently applied and its motivation goal as a completed opening. The valid contract of the responsible unit determines this (Section~@ref{sec:assessment}). A child therefore retains recognition of its goals while the parent synthesis assessment is still open.
